<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sufetula</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sbeitla</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sufetula, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sbeitla">Sbeitla</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, was a Roman town that flourished from the 2nd century CE, known for its well-preserved forum with three temples to the Capitoline gods. In the 7th century it was the seat of the exarch <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-the-patrician">Gregory the Patrician</a>, who was defeated and killed near it by an Arab army in 647. The battle opened the way for Arab raids into Byzantine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>.</p></div>
